.TH	dhsServer 1 06/08/98 "NRC"

.SH NAME
dhsServer
\- A simulator for DHS servers.

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dhsServer
[
\-noStrict
] <
.B serverName
>

.SH DESCRIPTION
The 
.B dhsServer
executable simulates a DHS server.  This server accepts commands
and bulk data requests from a DHS client and sends responses back.  
All commands
and their attribute value list which are received by the 
\fBdhsServer\fR are displayed on standard out.
.P
The commands which are known to the \fBdhsServer\fR are:
.LP
.nf
.ta 4c 8c 12c
.ft B
\fBbdCtl\fR	\fBflush\fR	\fBset delay\fR
\fBstoInfo\fR	\fBtest Command1\fR	\fBtest Command2\fR
.fi
.ft R

Bulk data get requests result in a short buffer being returned.

Bulk data put requests of datasets result in the data being written
as an exported SDS structure to file server.out. Data from on-dataset put 
requests is discarded.


.SH OPERATION
The \fBserverName\fR must be be specified and is the name of the DHS Server
which is to be simulated.  
.P
If the \-noStrict command-line option is specified then strict
error checking is not done.  This means that error responses are
not returned for commands that are not recognized by the
\fBdhsServer\fR

If the client is on a different host, the IMP \fBmaster\fR task must
be run on both the client and server host before \fBdhsClient\fR is run.


.SH FILES
None

.SH EXAMPLES
.LP
An example of a command line with strict error checking:
.IP
.B example> dhsServer commandServer

.LP
An example of a command lines without strict error checking:
.IP
.B  example> dhsServer \-noStrict commandServer


.SH  ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
IMP_SCRATCH controls the directory in which the IMP scratch files are created. 
This must match the IMP_SCRATCH environment variable used when running the 
client if the client is on the same host, or when running the IMP \fBmaster\fR
task if the client is on a different host.


